<h2>1. Jollof Rice</h2>
<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Jollof_rice.jpg">
<p>
Jollof rice is Nigeria’s most popular dish, widely served at parties and celebrations. 
Its rich tomato base and spices make it a favorite across the country.
</p>

<h2>2. Egusi Soup and Pounded Yam</h2>
<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Pounded_yam_and_egusi_soup.jpg">
<p>
A traditional meal known for its thick texture and rich flavor, commonly eaten in 
many Nigerian households.
</p>

<h2>3. Suya</h2>
<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Suya.jpg">
<p>
Suya is a spicy grilled meat sold mostly at night. It remains one of Nigeria’s most 
popular street foods.
</p>

<h2>4. Moi Moi</h2>
<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Moi_moi.jpg">
<p>
Moi Moi is a steamed bean pudding rich in protein, often served with rice or eaten alone.
</p>

<h2>5. Akara</h2>
<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Akara.jpg">
<p>
Akara is a deep-fried bean cake commonly eaten for breakfast across Nigeria.
</p>

